1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:06,973
How a 167-Year-Old Bank Died in a
Weekend Friday, March 17, 2023.

2
00:00:06,973 --> 00:00:09,533
Credit Suisse is one of the
biggest banks in the world.

3
00:00:09,533 --> 00:00:11,775
It is one hundred and
sixty-seven years old.

4
00:00:11,935 --> 00:00:13,855
It helped build Switzerland.

5
00:00:14,495 --> 00:00:16,015
Sunday night, it's gone.

6
00:00:16,015 --> 00:00:19,375
Sold to its oldest rival for about
three billion Swiss francs.

7
00:00:19,375 --> 00:00:22,175
And sixteen billion francs of
its bonds are wiped out.

8
00:00:22,175 --> 00:00:23,375
Erased.

9
00:00:23,741 --> 00:00:26,381
A bank that old doesn't
die in one weekend.

10
00:00:26,381 --> 00:00:27,381
Not really.

11
00:00:27,261 --> 00:00:29,741
So we're going to follow the
money out the door.

12
00:00:29,741 --> 00:00:32,941
Where it went, who paid, and
who walked away richer.

13
00:00:32,941 --> 00:00:34,700
This is Billions and Bulls.

14
00:00:34,700 --> 00:00:36,700
Let's follow the money.

15
00:00:37,900 --> 00:00:40,140
Part 1 The Railway Bank

16
00:00:40,140 --> 00:00:42,890
The story starts in 1856, in Zurich.

17
00:00:42,890 --> 00:00:44,650
Switzerland wanted railways.

18
00:00:44,650 --> 00:00:48,570
And a politician and businessman named
Alfred Escher wanted them built with

19
00:00:48,570 --> 00:00:51,130
Swiss money, not French or German money.

20
00:00:51,130 --> 00:00:52,987
So he started a bank to pay for them.

21
00:00:52,987 --> 00:00:55,787
It was called the
Schweizerische Kreditanstalt.

22
00:00:55,867 --> 00:00:58,987
Later, the world would know
it as Credit Suisse.

23
00:00:59,387 --> 00:01:00,955
The idea was popular.

24
00:01:01,115 --> 00:01:05,195
Within days, investors asked for shares
worth many times what the bank was

25
00:01:05,195 --> 00:01:06,195
offering.

26
00:01:05,915 --> 00:01:09,675
In its early years, about a quarter of its
revenue came from a single railway

27
00:01:09,675 --> 00:01:10,675
company.

28
00:01:10,475 --> 00:01:12,500
One that Escher himself ran.

29
00:01:12,740 --> 00:01:15,860
That's how close the bank was
to power from day one.

30
00:01:15,860 --> 00:01:19,220
The man who ran the railway also ran
the bank that lent to it.

31
00:01:19,220 --> 00:01:23,060
Later, he led the company that built the
Gotthard line, the railway through

32
00:01:23,060 --> 00:01:24,364
the Alps.

33
00:01:24,844 --> 00:01:27,484
From there, the bank grew
with the country.

34
00:01:27,484 --> 00:01:31,324
It moved into a grand building on
Paradeplatz, the square at the heart of

35
00:01:31,324 --> 00:01:32,364
Swiss banking.

36
00:01:32,364 --> 00:01:34,963
It opened an office in New York in 1870.

37
00:01:35,123 --> 00:01:38,563
And over the decades, it bought its way
into Wall Street, including the

38
00:01:38,563 --> 00:01:41,363
American investment bank First Boston.

39
00:01:41,843 --> 00:01:45,123
By the 2000s, Credit Suisse
was two banks in one.

40
00:01:45,123 --> 00:01:46,123
A Swiss bank.

41
00:01:46,308 --> 00:01:50,388
Safe, quiet, managing the money of rich
families from all over the world.

42
00:01:50,388 --> 00:01:51,908
And an investment bank.

43
00:01:51,908 --> 00:01:52,948
Bigger risks.

44
00:01:52,948 --> 00:01:54,068
Bigger bonuses.

45
00:01:54,068 --> 00:01:55,627
Bigger swings.

46
00:01:55,947 --> 00:02:01,307
When the financial crisis hit in 2008, its
Swiss rival, UBS, needed a rescue

47
00:02:01,307 --> 00:02:02,427
from the Swiss state.

48
00:02:02,427 --> 00:02:03,755
Credit Suisse didn't.

49
00:02:03,914 --> 00:02:07,514
It raised money from private investors
instead, and came out looking like

50
00:02:07,514 --> 00:02:09,835
the stronger of the two.

51
00:02:09,914 --> 00:02:10,914
Remember that.

52
00:02:10,874 --> 00:02:14,357
Because fifteen years later, the
roles would flip completely.

53
00:02:14,837 --> 00:02:16,357
So what went wrong?

54
00:02:16,357 --> 00:02:17,637
It wasn't one thing.

55
00:02:17,637 --> 00:02:18,917
It was a long list.

56
00:02:18,917 --> 00:02:21,957
And it starts with a loan to
a fishing company.

57
00:02:22,997 --> 00:02:25,362
Part 2 A Decade of Scandals

58
00:02:25,601 --> 00:02:30,322
Between 2013 and 2016, Credit Suisse helped
arrange about one billion dollars

59
00:02:30,322 --> 00:02:33,921
in loans and bonds for state-linked
companies in Mozambique.

60
00:02:33,921 --> 00:02:36,630
On paper, a lot of it was for
a tuna fishing fleet.

61
00:02:36,790 --> 00:02:40,070
Much of the money was diverted, through
kickbacks to Credit Suisse bankers

62
00:02:40,070 --> 00:02:41,578
and Mozambican officials.

63
00:02:41,578 --> 00:02:46,458
In 2021, the bank agreed to pay about four
hundred and seventy-five million

64
00:02:46,458 --> 00:02:48,778
dollars to American and
British authorities.

65
00:02:49,418 --> 00:02:51,932
In 2019, it got stranger.

66
00:02:51,932 --> 00:02:56,092
Credit Suisse was caught hiring private
detectives to follow a former top

67
00:02:56,092 --> 00:02:58,519
executive, who had left to join UBS.

68
00:02:58,519 --> 00:03:03,079
The Swiss regulator later found that the bank
had planned seven spying operations

69
00:03:03,079 --> 00:03:05,799
between 2016 and 2019.

70
00:03:06,166 --> 00:03:10,566
The chief executive, Tidjane
Thiam, resigned in early 2020.

71
00:03:11,286 --> 00:03:13,206
Then came 2021.

72
00:03:13,286 --> 00:03:14,871
The year it all got expensive.

73
00:03:14,871 --> 00:03:19,352
In March, Credit Suisse froze about ten
billion dollars in investment funds

74
00:03:19,352 --> 00:03:23,191
linked to Greensill Capital, a lending
firm that collapsed days later.

75
00:03:23,191 --> 00:03:26,643
More than a thousand investors
couldn't get their money out.

76
00:03:27,122 --> 00:03:31,842
Just weeks after that, a private
investment firm called Archegos imploded.

77
00:03:31,842 --> 00:03:34,422
Credit Suisse had lent it
huge amounts of money.

78
00:03:34,422 --> 00:03:38,155
When Archegos couldn't pay, Credit
Suisse booked a charge of four point

79
00:03:38,155 --> 00:03:41,941
four billion Swiss francs, and
warned of more losses to come.

80
00:03:41,941 --> 00:03:44,275
Other banks lost money on Archegos too.

81
00:03:44,275 --> 00:03:46,434
None lost as much as Credit Suisse.

82
00:03:46,434 --> 00:03:49,496
And that's a story we'll
follow in its own episode.

83
00:03:49,816 --> 00:03:54,856
In January 2022, the bank's new chairman
resigned, after an internal probe

84
00:03:54,856 --> 00:03:57,959
reportedly found he had broken
COVID quarantine rules.

85
00:03:58,119 --> 00:04:02,919
And in June 2022, a Swiss court convicted
Credit Suisse of failing to stop

86
00:04:02,919 --> 00:04:06,610
a Bulgarian cocaine gang from
laundering money through its accounts.

87
00:04:06,610 --> 00:04:10,530
It was reportedly the first criminal
trial of a major Swiss bank in the

88
00:04:10,530 --> 00:04:11,730
country.

89
00:04:11,810 --> 00:04:13,970
None of these alone could kill a bank.

90
00:04:13,970 --> 00:04:16,467
But each one sent the same
message to clients.

91
00:04:16,707 --> 00:04:19,027
Something here isn't working.

92
00:04:19,347 --> 00:04:23,747
And in banking, that message is the
most expensive one there is.

93
00:04:24,467 --> 00:04:26,829
Part 3 The Slow-Motion Run

94
00:04:27,149 --> 00:04:28,909
Here's the thing about a bank.

95
00:04:28,909 --> 00:04:30,669
It doesn't keep your money in a vault.

96
00:04:30,669 --> 00:04:31,709
It lends it out.

97
00:04:31,709 --> 00:04:32,989
It invests it.

98
00:04:32,989 --> 00:04:36,829
At any moment, only a small part
is available as cash.

99
00:04:36,829 --> 00:04:38,376
That works perfectly.

100
00:04:38,616 --> 00:04:43,256
As long as everyone doesn't ask for
their money back at the same time.

101
00:04:43,336 --> 00:04:47,653
In October 2022, Credit Suisse
announced a radical rescue plan.

102
00:04:47,892 --> 00:04:49,893
Cut about nine thousand jobs.

103
00:04:49,893 --> 00:04:51,572
Split off the investment bank.

104
00:04:51,572 --> 00:04:54,299
Raise around four billion
francs in new capital.

105
00:04:54,458 --> 00:04:58,618
Its new anchor investor was the Saudi
National Bank, which agreed to put

106
00:04:58,618 --> 00:05:03,898
in up to one and a half billion dollars, for
a stake of just under ten percent.

107
00:05:04,219 --> 00:05:06,043
But clients were already leaving.

108
00:05:06,202 --> 00:05:10,923
In the last three months of 2022, they
pulled out one hundred and ten billion

109
00:05:10,923 --> 00:05:12,202
Swiss francs.

110
00:05:12,202 --> 00:05:16,362
That's more than a billion francs walking
out the door, every single day,

111
00:05:16,362 --> 00:05:17,440
for three months.

112
00:05:17,440 --> 00:05:22,080
For the full year, the bank lost seven
point three billion francs.

113
00:05:22,640 --> 00:05:24,960
Then came March 2023.

114
00:05:24,960 --> 00:05:29,371
On March 10, a bank in California,
Silicon Valley Bank, collapsed.

115
00:05:29,371 --> 00:05:33,371
Its customers had tried to pull out about
forty-two billion dollars in a

116
00:05:33,371 --> 00:05:34,371
single day.

117
00:05:34,331 --> 00:05:37,290
With an app, a run no longer takes weeks.

118
00:05:37,290 --> 00:05:38,489
It takes hours.

119
00:05:38,649 --> 00:05:43,209
Suddenly, every investor in the world was
looking for the next weak bank.

120
00:05:43,209 --> 00:05:47,338
On March 14, Credit Suisse published
its delayed annual report.

121
00:05:47,498 --> 00:05:52,538
It admitted "material weaknesses" in how it
checked its own financial reporting.

122
00:05:52,938 --> 00:05:56,538
The next morning, the chairman of the Saudi
National Bank was asked on television

123
00:05:56,538 --> 00:05:58,445
if his bank would put in more money.

124
00:05:58,685 --> 00:06:03,325
His answer: "Absolutely not." He
explained that going above ten percent

125
00:06:03,325 --> 00:06:05,485
would trigger new rules from regulators.

126
00:06:05,485 --> 00:06:07,005
It was a technical reason.

127
00:06:07,005 --> 00:06:08,685
But markets didn't hear the reason.

128
00:06:08,685 --> 00:06:13,075
They heard the word "not." Credit Suisse
shares fell as much as twenty-five

129
00:06:13,075 --> 00:06:16,116
percent that day, to a record low.

130
00:06:16,435 --> 00:06:19,795
That night, the Swiss National Bank said
it would lend Credit Suisse up

131
00:06:19,795 --> 00:06:21,779
to fifty billion francs.

132
00:06:21,779 --> 00:06:24,739
The next morning, Credit Suisse
said it would take the money.

133
00:06:24,739 --> 00:06:26,898
The shares bounced, for about a day.

134
00:06:26,898 --> 00:06:27,898
It wasn't enough.

135
00:06:27,897 --> 00:06:32,697
In the first three months of 2023, clients
pulled another sixty-one billion

136
00:06:32,697 --> 00:06:33,697
francs.

137
00:06:33,338 --> 00:06:37,572
And the bank said the heaviest losses
came in the second half of March.

138
00:06:37,892 --> 00:06:41,812
By Friday, the people in charge knew the
bank would not survive another

139
00:06:41,812 --> 00:06:43,012
week.

140
00:06:43,892 --> 00:06:46,052
Part 4 The Weekend

141
00:06:46,052 --> 00:06:47,212
Why a weekend?

142
00:06:47,451 --> 00:06:50,331
Because banks are safest when
markets are closed.

143
00:06:50,331 --> 00:06:53,851
The Swiss had until Monday morning, when
trading would open in Asia, to

144
00:06:53,851 --> 00:06:54,971
find an answer.

145
00:06:54,971 --> 00:06:57,931
Otherwise, the run could
turn into a stampede.

146
00:06:58,672 --> 00:07:02,432
So, over the weekend, the Swiss
government, the central bank, the financial

147
00:07:02,432 --> 00:07:05,105
regulator and UBS sat down to make a deal.

148
00:07:05,105 --> 00:07:09,185
At Friday's close, Credit Suisse had been
worth around eight billion dollars

149
00:07:09,185 --> 00:07:12,145
on the stock market,
according to news reports.

150
00:07:12,545 --> 00:07:15,387
On Sunday evening, March 19,
they announced it.

151
00:07:15,627 --> 00:07:17,867
UBS would take over Credit Suisse.

152
00:07:17,867 --> 00:07:21,787
The price: three billion Swiss
francs, paid in UBS shares.

153
00:07:21,787 --> 00:07:25,430
That's about seventy-six centimes
for each Credit Suisse share.

154
00:07:25,750 --> 00:07:30,390
Less than one franc, for a share that
had once traded for far more.

155
00:07:30,470 --> 00:07:33,510
To make it work, the government
used emergency powers.

156
00:07:33,510 --> 00:07:35,828
Shareholders of both banks
didn't get a vote.

157
00:07:35,828 --> 00:07:39,267
The Swiss state also offered
UBS a safety net.

158
00:07:39,267 --> 00:07:43,347
If the riskiest parts of Credit Suisse
lost money, UBS would take the first

159
00:07:43,347 --> 00:07:45,423
five billion francs of losses.

160
00:07:45,662 --> 00:07:48,143
The government would cover
the next nine billion.

161
00:07:48,143 --> 00:07:52,062
And the Swiss National Bank promised up
to one hundred billion francs in

162
00:07:52,062 --> 00:07:55,822
extra loans, backed by the government,
so the combined bank would never

163
00:07:55,822 --> 00:07:57,423
run out of cash.

164
00:07:57,983 --> 00:07:59,982
There were other options on the table.

165
00:07:59,982 --> 00:08:03,742
The government reportedly considered
taking over Credit Suisse itself, at

166
00:08:03,742 --> 00:08:04,942
least for a while.

167
00:08:04,942 --> 00:08:08,622
But that would have put the whole bank, and
all its risks, on the taxpayers'

168
00:08:08,622 --> 00:08:09,622
shoulders.

169
00:08:09,224 --> 00:08:12,744
A sale to UBS looked like
the smaller risk.

170
00:08:13,144 --> 00:08:14,744
Remember 2008?

171
00:08:14,744 --> 00:08:16,984
UBS was the bank that needed a rescue.

172
00:08:16,984 --> 00:08:19,811
Now, fifteen years later,
UBS was the rescuer.

173
00:08:19,811 --> 00:08:23,731
And Credit Suisse, the bank that had
stood on its own, was the one being

174
00:08:23,731 --> 00:08:24,731
saved.

175
00:08:24,451 --> 00:08:26,211
Or rather, swallowed.

176
00:08:26,691 --> 00:08:29,580
But the biggest shock of the
weekend wasn't the price.

177
00:08:29,580 --> 00:08:33,020
It was what happened to a group of
investors most people had never heard

178
00:08:33,020 --> 00:08:34,220
of.

179
00:08:34,300 --> 00:08:35,300
Quick thing.

180
00:08:35,180 --> 00:08:37,740
If you like videos that follow
the money, subscribe.

181
00:08:37,740 --> 00:08:38,740
It's free.

182
00:08:38,472 --> 00:08:40,472
And unlike some bonds in this story...

183
00:08:40,472 --> 00:08:42,472
it won't go to zero.

184
00:08:43,592 --> 00:08:46,232
Part 5 The Bonds That Went to Zero

185
00:08:46,232 --> 00:08:50,100
After the 2008 crisis, regulators
wanted banks to have a cushion.

186
00:08:50,340 --> 00:08:53,940
Something that would absorb losses
before taxpayers had to step in.

187
00:08:53,940 --> 00:08:57,380
One answer was a special kind
of bond, called AT1.

188
00:08:57,380 --> 00:08:59,380
Additional Tier 1.

189
00:08:59,700 --> 00:09:00,829
Here's how it works.

190
00:09:00,909 --> 00:09:04,509
Investors lend the bank money, and get a
higher interest rate than on normal

191
00:09:04,509 --> 00:09:05,509
bonds.

192
00:09:05,230 --> 00:09:07,229
In exchange, they accept a risk.

193
00:09:07,229 --> 00:09:11,229
If the bank gets into deep trouble, the
bonds can be converted into shares,

194
00:09:11,229 --> 00:09:12,259
or written off.

195
00:09:12,259 --> 00:09:15,618
That's why people in the market
also call them CoCos.

196
00:09:15,618 --> 00:09:17,298
Contingent convertibles.

197
00:09:17,298 --> 00:09:20,327
They're bonds that can stop being bonds.

198
00:09:20,487 --> 00:09:24,647
Credit Suisse's AT1 bonds had a
clause for exactly this moment.

199
00:09:24,647 --> 00:09:27,927
If the bank received extraordinary support
from the government, they could

200
00:09:27,927 --> 00:09:29,927
be wiped out.

201
00:09:30,007 --> 00:09:31,553
That's exactly what happened.

202
00:09:31,553 --> 00:09:36,112
The Swiss regulator ordered Credit Suisse's
AT1 bonds, about sixteen billion

203
00:09:36,112 --> 00:09:38,272
francs of them, written down to zero.

204
00:09:38,272 --> 00:09:39,392
Not a discount.

205
00:09:39,392 --> 00:09:40,592
Zero.

206
00:09:41,152 --> 00:09:43,366
But here's why investors were furious.

207
00:09:43,526 --> 00:09:46,166
Normally, shareholders are
first in line to lose.

208
00:09:46,166 --> 00:09:48,726
They own the company, so they
take the first hit.

209
00:09:48,726 --> 00:09:52,006
Bondholders lose only after the
shareholders are wiped out.

210
00:09:52,006 --> 00:09:54,458
At Credit Suisse, it was the
other way around.

211
00:09:54,618 --> 00:09:58,458
Shareholders still got about three
billion francs in UBS shares.

212
00:09:58,458 --> 00:10:01,579
The AT1 bondholders got nothing.

213
00:10:02,058 --> 00:10:05,658
The Swiss said the bonds' own terms
allowed it, once the government stepped

214
00:10:05,658 --> 00:10:06,658
in.

215
00:10:06,174 --> 00:10:08,655
The rest of the market panicked anyway.

216
00:10:08,655 --> 00:10:13,214
On Monday, AT1 bonds of other banks around
the world fell sharply, as investors

217
00:10:13,214 --> 00:10:15,572
wondered if their own bonds could be next.

218
00:10:15,572 --> 00:10:19,973
But the very next day, regulators in the
European Union put out a statement.

219
00:10:19,973 --> 00:10:23,812
In Europe, they said,
shareholders absorb losses first.

220
00:10:23,812 --> 00:10:25,259
AT1 comes after.

221
00:10:25,418 --> 00:10:28,378
The Bank of England said much the same.

222
00:10:28,458 --> 00:10:31,978
Around three thousand bondholders went to
court in Switzerland, filing more

223
00:10:31,978 --> 00:10:34,024
than three hundred and sixty complaints.

224
00:10:34,183 --> 00:10:38,504
And in October 2025, the Federal
Administrative Court ruled that the write-down

225
00:10:38,504 --> 00:10:40,583
lacked a sufficient legal basis.

226
00:10:40,583 --> 00:10:42,152
But that wasn't the end.

227
00:10:42,152 --> 00:10:46,392
The regulator and UBS appealed to
Switzerland's Federal Supreme Court.

228
00:10:46,392 --> 00:10:50,792
As this video was made, the bonds were
still worth zero, waiting for a final

229
00:10:50,792 --> 00:10:51,992
answer.

230
00:10:52,910 --> 00:10:55,710
Part 6 Who Paid, and Who Profited

231
00:10:55,710 --> 00:10:57,790
So let's add it up.

232
00:10:57,790 --> 00:11:01,261
Credit Suisse shareholders
lost almost everything.

233
00:11:01,421 --> 00:11:05,021
Shares that had been worth billions
became a few centimes each.

234
00:11:05,021 --> 00:11:08,781
AT1 bondholders lost about
sixteen billion francs.

235
00:11:08,781 --> 00:11:09,901
At least for now.

236
00:11:09,901 --> 00:11:12,369
And thousands of employees
lost their jobs.

237
00:11:12,529 --> 00:11:16,469
Since the takeover, UBS has reportedly
cut about seventeen thousand five

238
00:11:16,469 --> 00:11:17,469
hundred

239
00:11:17,569 --> 00:11:18,569
positions.

240
00:11:18,369 --> 00:11:19,513
The name went too.

241
00:11:19,593 --> 00:11:24,713
On May 31, 2024, Credit Suisse was
legally merged into UBS.

242
00:11:24,713 --> 00:11:28,393
After one hundred and sixty-eight years,
the bank founded to build Swiss

243
00:11:28,393 --> 00:11:30,593
railways simply stopped existing.

244
00:11:30,752 --> 00:11:35,072
On a Friday, quietly, with no
weekend drama at all.

245
00:11:35,152 --> 00:11:37,312
What about Swiss taxpayers?

246
00:11:37,312 --> 00:11:38,976
This is where it gets surprising.

247
00:11:39,136 --> 00:11:43,937
In August 2023, UBS ended the
government's nine-billion-franc guarantee,

248
00:11:43,937 --> 00:11:45,937
and the central bank backstop.

249
00:11:45,937 --> 00:11:47,857
It said it didn't need them anymore.

250
00:11:47,857 --> 00:11:50,202
The government says it
didn't lose a franc.

251
00:11:50,441 --> 00:11:54,521
It actually collected around two
hundred million francs in fees.

252
00:11:54,921 --> 00:11:56,361
And UBS?

253
00:11:56,361 --> 00:12:00,761
In the second quarter of 2023, it
reported a profit of about twenty-nine

254
00:12:00,761 --> 00:12:01,901
billion dollars.

255
00:12:02,061 --> 00:12:06,381
Almost all of it came from one accounting
line, called negative goodwill.

256
00:12:06,381 --> 00:12:10,701
In plain English: UBS paid far less for
Credit Suisse than its assets were

257
00:12:10,701 --> 00:12:11,787
worth on paper.

258
00:12:11,947 --> 00:12:14,427
The difference showed up as profit.

259
00:12:14,907 --> 00:12:16,427
But the story isn't over.

260
00:12:16,427 --> 00:12:17,427
It moved.

261
00:12:17,307 --> 00:12:20,799
Switzerland now has one giant
bank instead of two.

262
00:12:20,959 --> 00:12:25,519
If UBS ever got into trouble, there's
no rival left to rescue it.

263
00:12:25,519 --> 00:12:29,584
So lawmakers want UBS to hold
much more capital.

264
00:12:29,824 --> 00:12:34,784
In September 2026, Switzerland's upper
house backed a plan that UBS says

265
00:12:34,784 --> 00:12:38,258
could cost it about sixteen billion
dollars in extra capital.

266
00:12:38,498 --> 00:12:42,658
UBS has pushed back hard, and has
reportedly looked at options that include

267
00:12:42,658 --> 00:12:44,418
moving its headquarters.

268
00:12:44,784 --> 00:12:48,864
A Swiss parliamentary inquiry put the
main blame on Credit Suisse's own

269
00:12:48,864 --> 00:12:50,549
managers, over many years.

270
00:12:50,549 --> 00:12:54,229
It also criticized the regulator, for
giving the bank too much room on its

271
00:12:54,229 --> 00:12:56,230
capital back in 2017.

272
00:12:57,109 --> 00:13:01,529
So, how does a one hundred and
sixty-seven-year-old bank die in a weekend?

273
00:13:02,089 --> 00:13:03,209
It doesn't.

274
00:13:03,209 --> 00:13:05,209
The weekend was only the funeral.

275
00:13:05,209 --> 00:13:07,209
The money left over years.

276
00:13:07,209 --> 00:13:08,649
Scandal by scandal.

277
00:13:08,649 --> 00:13:10,089
Loss by loss.

278
00:13:10,089 --> 00:13:11,609
One client at a time.

279
00:13:11,609 --> 00:13:13,102
A bank runs on money.

280
00:13:13,261 --> 00:13:14,941
But money runs on trust.

281
00:13:14,941 --> 00:13:18,221
And when the trust leaves,
the money follows.

282
00:13:19,341 --> 00:13:22,621
Next time, we follow the money
much closer to home.

283
00:13:22,621 --> 00:13:23,870
Your savings account.

284
00:13:24,110 --> 00:13:27,870
Why does your bank pay you almost
nothing, while it earns so much more on

285
00:13:27,870 --> 00:13:28,870
your money?

